.TH ECHO 1
.SH NAME
echo \- echo arguments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "echo [ \-n ] [ arg ] ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B echo
writes its arguments separated by blanks and terminated by a newline on the standard output.
If the flag
.B \-n
is used, no newline is added to the output.
.PP
.B echo
is useful for producing diagnostics in shell programs and for writing constant data on pipes.
To send diagnostics to the standard error file, do
`echo ... 1>&2'.
.PP
.B echo
exists as a built-in command when running the C shell
.RB ( csh ),
and has slightly different options.
See
.BR csh (1)
for details.
.SH EXAMPLES
Echo the word ``\fItree\fR'' into the file
.IR list :
.RS
.nf
echo tree >list
.fi
.RE
Print the names of the files in the current directory:
.RS
.nf
echo *
.fi
.RE
Print the name of the home directory:
.RS
.nf
echo $HOME
.fi
.RE
.sp
